Chain fully confirmed — and it's better-call's parser, not the browser, that makes it deterministic-but-wrong:

```js
const key = str.slice(index, eqIdx).trim();
if (!cookies.has(key)) {          // <-- FIRST occurrence wins, later duplicates discarded
  cookies.set(key, tryDecode(val));
}
```

So: browser orders oldest-first per RFC 6265 §5.4 → better-call keeps the first → better-auth reads that one → an older stale twin permanently shadows every later sign-in. That matches the prod measurement exactly (`dead; good` → null, `good; dead` → session).

The proper fix is available through the public API: `auth.api.getSession({ headers })` takes any `Headers`, so we can hand it a header carrying exactly one candidate and resolve each in turn. No internals, no monkey-patching. Setting up a separate worktree — this changes the auth read path, which is a different concern from #2558's write-path convergence.

